      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We present our experience with uniportal videothoracoscopic surgery (VATS-U), examining its indications, limits, and results."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Since January 2009, 66 patients underwent VATS-U for the following indications: pneumothorax (n = 25), lung nodule (n = 15; n = 10 with preoperative radiolocalization), wedge biopsy (n = 15), hyperhidrosis (n = 10), and chest wall schwannoma (n = 1). The conversion rate to conventional video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS), postoperative pain, complications, residual paraesthesia, and hospitalization were analyzed. Operative time, postoperative pain, and paraesthesia were retrospectively compared with a cohort of 172 cases of conventional multiportal VATS, performed in the same period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Conversion to traditional VATS was necessary in two cases (pulmonary nodule, n = 1; pneumothorax, n = 1). The mean pain score was 0.8, the mean operation time was 42 minutes, and 10 patients had postoperative paraesthesia that lasted a mean of 7 days. No postoperative complications were reported, and the mean postoperative hospital stay was 3 days (range, 1-6 days). The comparison between the VATS-U and the standard multiportal VATS group showed in the VATS-U group a lower but not statistically significant pain score and paraesthesia as well as a lower and statistically significant operative time."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Uniportal videothoracoscopic surgery has a wide range of indications: lung apex resections and pleurodesis for spontaneous pneumothorax treatment; pulmonary nodule assessment with or without preoperative localization; lung biopsy for interstitial diseases; unilateral or bilateral sympathectomy to treat hyperhidrosis; benign chest wall tumor evaluation. The limits of this technique are linked to pleural adhesions or lung nodules in difficult positions. In our experience, VATS-U results in minimal postoperative pain, allowing for fast functional recovery and a consequent short hospital stay; thus, we suggest that VATS-U is a valid alternative to traditional multiportal VATS for indications beyond cosmetic benefits. Prospective randomized trials are necessary to validate the advantages of uniportal VATS."
        }
      ]},
